ng"}] "MYA"]]] [:tr {:class "hidden_referees", :style "display: none"} [:th {} "Judge / Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5001859"} "Ed Devery"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]]] [:tr {:class "hidden_referees", :style "display: none"} [:th {} "Judge / Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002547"} "I. Gruia"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ROU"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ROU.png"}] "ROU"]]] [:tr {:class "hidden_referees", :style "display: none"} [:th {} "Judge / Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002985"} "Károly Kovács"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "After wins by Argentine and American boxers in 1948 and 1952 the balance of power in the flyweight division appeared to have shifted towards Europe by the time of the Melbourne Olympics. Amongst those favoured to make an impression in Melbourne were two European champions, the 1955 gold medallist " [:a {:href "/athletes/3589"} "Edgar Basel"] " of Germany and " [:a {:href "/athletes/7743"} "Henryk Kukier"] " who had won gold for Poland in 1953. " [:a {:href "/athletes/8554"} "Vladimir Stolnikov"] " of the USSR and " [:a {:href "/athletes/7904"} "Mircea Dobrescu"] " of Romania were also considered as medal contenders. With 19 boxers entered only three first round fights were needed but the luck of the draw pitted Stolnikov and Basel together and the German, who had reached the Olympic final in 1952, was eliminated by the Soviet. Stolnikov’s run was surprisingly ended by the teenaged Briton, " [:a {:href "/athletes/3499"} "Terry Spinks"] ". Spinks was a late addition to the British team having originally been left out due to his youth and a perceived lack of experience. A press campaign orchestrated by the journalist Reg Gutteridge had helped change the selectors’ minds. The Londoner proved a revelation as he used his lightning hand speed to pepper his rivals with a succession of quick fire punches and he comfortably outpointed  Dobrescu in the final to become the first British fighter to win an Olympic title since 1924."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Ironically Spinks’ Olympic success may have adversely affected his professional career. He was matched against older and more experienced opponents from early in the career and, although he did become British champion, he walked away from the sport at the age of just 24 after a series of hard fights.  More successful as professionals were Irish bronze medallist " [:a {:href "/athletes/3946"} "Johnny Caldwell"] " and Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/4080"} "Salvatore Burruni"] " and both became world champions."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/3499"} "Terry Spinks"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/7904"} "Mircea Dobrescu"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ROU"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ROU.png"}] "ROU"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/3946"} "Johnny Caldwell"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/IRL"} [:i